Implement the following changes without deleting the existing Kubernetes code.

1. Global Kubernetes Feature Flag

* Add a backend configuration flag, for example:
    KUBERNETES_ENABLED=true/false
* Default must be false.
* When false, Kubernetes functionality should work exactly as it does today.
* When true, Kubernetes must be completely disabled.

2. Complete Kubernetes Disablement

When KUBERNETES_ENABLED=true:

* Do not make any Kubernetes API/client calls, including background calls.
* Do not initialize Kubernetes clients unnecessarily.
* Do not fetch Kubernetes deployment/configuration data.
* Previous Release Branch should use the existing non-Kubernetes fallback mechanism.
* Deployment and Config Changes sections must be completely hidden from the UI.
* Release Readiness Kubernetes-related UI must be hidden.
* Team Administration must hide Kubernetes configuration fields/options.
* Frontend and backend must both respect the same flag.
* Do not delete Kubernetes code; control its execution through the feature flag.
* Ensure no indirect/background Kubernetes call happens while the feature is disabled.

When the flag is changed back to false and the application is restarted, all existing Kubernetes functionality must work normally again.

3. Show Skipped Components

On Release Readiness → Submit Analysis:

* Fix the Show Skipped switch.
* When enabled, all applicable skipped components must be displayed correctly.
* Ensure this works without affecting the existing non-skipped component behavior.

4. Validation

Verify both modes:

* KUBERNETES_ENABLED=true → zero Kubernetes calls and all Kubernetes UI/functionality hidden.
* KUBERNETES_ENABLED=false → existing Kubernetes behavior works normally.
* Local and Azure deployment configuration must support the flag correctly.
* No existing non-Kubernetes functionality should be impacted.
